---
title: HTTP Status Codes
slug: guides/http-status-codes
---

Immersve APIs respond with 200, 400, 401, 403, 404, or 429 HTTP status codes.
In rare cases endpoints may respond with status codes in the 5xx range. Every
response, whatever its status code, also carries an `X-Request-Id` header that
identifies the request; see *Request IDs*.

Please note that certain legacy or deprecated API endpoints could
operate slightly outside the norms described below. Any such exceptions will
be documented on the endpoints.

## 200 OK

Your request was successfully processed, enjoy!

## 400 Malformed request

This code indicates a syntax error in your request and that adjustments are
required on your end. Usually, the response body will contain more information
on what went wrong.

Don't try again, as it will result in the same response.

### Troubleshooting
- Make sure your HTTP body fields are set correctly.
- Check query parameters are set correctly.
- Check path parameters are set correctly.

## 401 Unauthorized
API key or JWT is missing, expired or invalid. Refer to our
{% link page="guides/authentication" /%} guide for more information.

## 403 Forbidden
Resource is missing, permission is denied or there is a business rule
violation.

### Resource missing or permission denied
- Check the resource id is correct.
- Check your user or API key has membership for the account that owns the
resource you are accessing.
- Check your user or API key's roles for the account allow the required action.

### Business rule violated
In cases where access to a resource is granted but a request is still forbidden
due to a business rule violation, a 403 status code is returned with additional
details in the `errorCode` field of the response body. Specific `errorCode`
values and their meanings will be documented on each endpoint.

```json {% title="Example 403 response for a business rule violation." %}
{
  "statusCode": 403,
  "statusName": "Forbidden",
  "errorCode": "INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS",
  "reason": "Withdrawal amount exceeds Funding Source balance"
}
```


## 404 Not Found
Similar to a 400 code but specific to scenarios where an incorrect URL or HTTP
method is used. Review our [API reference](/api-reference/) to ensure you are
making the correct request.

## 429 Too Many Requests
Rate limits have been exceeded. Refer to {% link page="guides/rate-limiting" /%} for more information.

## 5xx Server Error
Errors in the 5xx range suggest a temporary problem on Immersve's side. Clients
may retry these requests, but should use bounded retries with increasing delay/backoff.
Do not retry indefinitely or in a tight loop, as excessive retries may be rate
limited with 429 Too Many Requests. For non-idempotent requests, ensure your 
integration can safely handle duplicate attempts before retrying.

Our engineering team monitors these errors closely but, if you experience
persistent errors, bug reports are also welcome at
[support@immersve.com](mailto:support@immersve.com). Include the `X-Request-Id`
response header value of a failing request so it can be traced directly; see
*Request IDs*.

## Request IDs

Every response from the Immersve API carries an `X-Request-Id` header. The
value is an identifier Immersve assigns to that one request. It is present on
successful responses and on error responses alike, including 401 and 429
responses rejected before the request reaches an endpoint.

Log the `X-Request-Id` value alongside each API call your integration makes.
When you report a problem to
[support@immersve.com](mailto:support@immersve.com), include the value for the
failing call. Immersve uses it to locate the request in its logs across every
internal service the call touched, which is faster and more reliable than
searching by timestamp and endpoint.

```http {% title="Example response headers showing the request ID." %}
HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden
Content-Type: application/json
X-Request-Id: Root=1-68cf3a1c-3f8e2b1a9c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d
```

{% note %}
Immersve generates the request ID. A value you send in an `X-Request-Id`
request header is ignored and is not echoed in the response, so it cannot be
used to correlate with Immersve support. Treat the value as an opaque string;
its format is not guaranteed and may change.
{% /note %}
